{"name":"Andes Ash","hex":"#c1a097","fetched":"2026-10-11","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"ash","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Symbol","definitions":["ISO 639-3 language code for Abishira."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The solid remains of a fire.","The nonaqueous remains of a material subjected to any complete oxidation process."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/ash"}],"entries":[{"title":"Andes","blocks":[{"text":"The Andes, Andes Mountains or Andean Mountain Range are the longest continental mountain range in the world, forming a continuous highland along the western edge of South America. The range is 8,900 kilometres (5,500 mi) long and 200 to 700 kilometres wide and has an average height of about 4,000 metres (13,000 ft). The Andes extend from south to north through seven South American countries: Argentina, Chile, Bolivia, Peru, Ecuador, Colombia, and Venezuela."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f3/Panoramic_view_Andes-Chile.jpg/330px-Panoramic_view_Andes-Chile.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Andes"},{"title":"Ash","blocks":[{"text":"Ash is the solid remnants of fires. Specifically, ash refers to all non-aqueous, non-gaseous residues that remain after something burns. In analytical chemistry, to analyse the mineral and metal content of chemical samples, ash is the non-gaseous, non-liquid residue after complete combustion."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/09/Wood_ash.jpg/330px-Wood_ash.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ash"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c8/Aconcagua_13.JPG/500px-Aconcagua_13.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c8/Aconcagua_13.JPG/1280px-Aconcagua_13.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c8/Aconcagua_13.JPG/500px-Aconcagua_13.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Aconcagua, in Argentina, the highest mountain in the Western Hemisphere and in the world outside the Himalayan system.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AAconcagua_13.JPG"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/77/ARG-2016-Aerial-Tierra_del_Fuego_%28Ushuaia%29%E2%80%93Valle_Carbajal_01.jpg/500px-ARG-2016-Aerial-Tierra_del_Fuego_%28Ushuaia%29%E2%80%93Valle_Carbajal_01.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/77/ARG-2016-Aerial-Tierra_del_Fuego_%28Ushuaia%29%E2%80%93Valle_Carbajal_01.jpg/1280px-ARG-2016-Aerial-Tierra_del_Fuego_%28Ushuaia%29%E2%80%93Valle_Carbajal_01.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/77/ARG-2016-Aerial-Tierra_del_Fuego_%28Ushuaia%29%E2%80%93Valle_Carbajal_01.jpg/500px-ARG-2016-Aerial-Tierra_del_Fuego_%28Ushuaia%29%E2%80%93Valle_Carbajal_01.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Aerial view of Valle Carbajal in the Tierra del Fuego. The Andes range is about 200 km (124 mi) wide throughout its length, except in the Bolivian flexure where it is about 640 kilometres (398 mi) wide.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AARG-2016-Aerial-Tierra_del_Fuego_(Ushuaia)%E2%80%93Valle_Carbajal_01.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/23/Cono_de_Arita%2C_Salta._%28Argentina%29.jpg/500px-Cono_de_Arita%2C_Salta._%28Argentina%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/23/Cono_de_Arita%2C_Salta._%28Argentina%29.jpg/1280px-Cono_de_Arita%2